We also estimate the trapping time within the current sheet by subtracting the ballistic transit time (the time the particle would take to traverse the sheet in the absence of scattering) from the total simulation time. For most interactions where significant scattering occurs, the particle remains trapped within the sheet for several gyroperiods, executing multiple bounces across the field reversal before escaping.

=== Accumulation across Multiple Current Sheets

To model transport through the heliosphere, we chain together a sequence of current sheet interactions separated by free-streaming intervals. Each particle is initialized with a random pitch angle $alpha$ and gyrophase $psi$. Between successive current sheets, the particle traverses a distance $d$ corresponding to the observed mean spatial separation between neighboring sheets. For simplicity, we assume (1) the current sheets are static during the interaction time; (2) $d$ is a constant depending only on the heliocentric radial distance; and (3) there is no pitch-angle scattering during transit. The transit time between sheets is $t_d = d \/ \| v_parallel \|$.

The total parallel displacement after $N$ interactions accumulates as
$ Delta z = sum_i upright("sgn") \( cos alpha_i \) dot.op d $
where $alpha_i$ is the pitch angle after the $i$-th interaction. This expression reflects the fact that the particle streams along the field line between sheets, with the sign determined by the direction of parallel motion.

The perpendicular displacement requires more careful treatment. In the heliosphere, the local magnetic field direction rotates with respect to the large-scale geometry---in particular, the radial direction---as the field follows the Parker spiral. Consequently, the perpendicular displacement acquired at one current sheet is oriented differently in the heliospheric frame from the displacement acquired at a distant sheet. We account for this slowly varying field-line orientation between current sheets by applying a random rotation $upright(bold(R))_i$ to each individual displacement, so that the accumulated perpendicular displacement is
$ Delta upright(bold(r))_perp = sum_i upright(bold(R))_i dot.op Delta upright(bold(r))_(perp \, i) $
where $Delta upright(bold(r))_(perp \, i)$ is the perpendicular displacement from the $i$-th interaction.

This scheme captures the essential physics while remaining computationally tractable: the expensive part (solving the Newton-Lorentz equation within each current sheet) is performed once per interaction, while the free-streaming intervals between sheets are handled analytically.

== Results

We propagate an ensemble of 512 particles and compute the mean-square displacements $chevron.l Delta z^2 chevron.r$ and $chevron.l Delta upright(bold(r))_perp^2 chevron.r$ by interpolating trajectories at successive time steps. Both parallel and perpendicular mean-square displacements grow linearly with time in the asymptotic limit, confirming diffusive behavior. The diffusion coefficients are extracted from the late-time behavior:
$ D_parallel = lim_(t arrow.r oo) chevron.l Delta z \( t \)^2 chevron.r \/ 2 t , #h(2em) D_perp = lim_(t arrow.r oo) chevron.l Delta upright(bold(r))_perp \( t \)^2 chevron.r \/ 2 t $
evaluated at $t = 2 times 10^3$ s, by which time the ensemble has undergone sufficiently many current sheet interactions for the transport to reach the diffusive regime.

