Several features of the trajectories merit discussion. The parallel displacement (left panel) grows approximately linearly with time for most particles, reflecting steady streaming along the magnetic field. However, occasional particles undergo abrupt direction reversals---visible as sharp kinks in the trajectory---where a single current sheet interaction produces a pitch-angle jump large enough to reverse the sign of $v_parallel$. These reflection events are a hallmark of the superfast scattering mechanism: in the quasi-linear picture, pitch-angle changes are small and incremental, so direction reversals require the cumulative effect of many scattering events, whereas current sheet interactions can produce them in a single step.

The perpendicular displacement (right panel) shows a qualitatively different character. Most of the time, the perpendicular position changes very little between current sheet encounters (consistent with our assumption of no cross-field transport during free streaming). At each current sheet interaction, however, the particle may undergo a discrete perpendicular jump $Delta upright(bold(r))_(perp \, i)$. The magnitude of these jumps varies widely: some interactions produce negligible cross-field displacement (when the particle remains magnetized throughout), while others produce jumps comparable to the gyroradius (when the particle becomes demagnetized, as illustrated in #ref(<fig-dR-perp>, supplement: [Figure])). The stochastic accumulation of these discrete jumps---each randomly oriented due to the random rotation ---produces a random walk that is macroscopically diffusive.

It is important to note that while individual interactions can produce large, non-diffusive jumps in both pitch angle and perpendicular position, the ensemble-averaged transport is diffusive in the long-time limit. This is because the jumps are statistically independent (each current sheet has randomly sampled parameters) and their accumulation satisfies the central limit theorem. The diffusion coefficients $D_parallel$ and $D_perp$ thus provide a valid characterization of the macroscopic transport, even though the microscopic scattering mechanism is fundamentally non-diffusive.

#ref(<fig-diffusion>, supplement: [Figure]) summarizes the diffusion coefficients as functions of particle energy for 1 AU and 0.1 AU environments, comparing our results with quasi-linear theory predictions derived from measured magnetic turbulence power spectra @chenParallelDiffusionCoefficient2024.

