The parallel diffusion coefficient ($D_parallel$, panel a) increases with particle energy at both heliocentric distances. At 1 AU, the current-sheet-driven $D_parallel$ is comparable in magnitude to the QLT prediction at low energies but diverges at higher energies. This behavior reflects the energy dependence of the scattering efficiency: as particle energy increases, the gyroradius grows relative to the sheet thickness, and the fraction of current sheets capable of producing strong scattering ($rho \/ L gt.tilde 1$) decreases. Consequently, higher-energy particles experience fewer effective scattering events per unit time, leading to a larger mean free path and larger $D_parallel$.

The perpendicular diffusion coefficient ($D_perp$, panel b) shows a steeper energy dependence than $D_parallel$. This arises because higher-energy particles, when they do interact strongly with a current sheet, undergo larger perpendicular displacements: the demagnetized gyroradius sets the scale of the cross-field jump, and this scale grows with particle energy. The combination of fewer but larger perpendicular jumps produces a net $D_perp$ that increases rapidly with energy.

The most significant result is the energy dependence of the ratio $D_perp \/ D_parallel$ (panel c). In contrast to the QLT-based prediction of a roughly constant ratio of $2 %$--$4 %$, our simulations show that $D_perp \/ D_parallel$ increases with particle energy, exceeding the typically assumed range for particles above $tilde.op 1$ MeV at 1 AU. This strong energy dependence arises from the distinct mechanisms governing parallel and perpendicular transport: parallel diffusion is controlled by pitch-angle scattering (which becomes less efficient at high energies as fewer sheets satisfy $rho \/ L gt.tilde 1$), while perpendicular diffusion is controlled by the magnitude of cross-field jumps during demagnetized interactions (which grows with energy). The two quantities thus have different energy scalings, producing a ratio that is far from constant.

This result differs from earlier studies suggesting the ratio is independent of both energy @shalchiPerpendicularDiffusionEnergetic2021 and intermittency @pucciEnergeticParticleTransport2016. Our findings offer a potential explanation for observational puzzles such as the large longitudinal and latitudinal extent of solar energetic particle (SEP) events detected by widely separated spacecraft @zhangPropagationSolarEnergetic2009@larioSolarEnergeticParticle2014@desaiLargeGradualSolar2016. If perpendicular diffusion is substantially more efficient at high energies than previously assumed, the broad spatial distributions of SEPs may be a natural consequence of current-sheet-mediated cross-field transport rather than requiring extended coronal injection or other non-standard mechanisms.

== Summary and Discussion

This study demonstrates that interactions with current sheets are a significant driver of energetic-particle transport in the heliosphere. By combining observed current sheet statistics with test-particle simulations, we have shown that:

+ Current sheet interactions produce both parallel transport (through pitch-angle scattering and reflection) and perpendicular transport (through cross-field jumps during demagnetization). Both transport channels operate through mechanisms that are qualitatively distinct from those assumed in quasi-linear theory.

+ The perpendicular-to-parallel diffusion ratio $D_perp \/ D_parallel$ is not constant but increases with particle energy, exceeding the typically assumed $2 %$--$4 %$ for particles above $tilde.op 1$ MeV at 1 AU. This energy dependence may be attributed to the perpendicular jump size scaling with the demagnetized gyroradius.

+ The current-sheet-driven transport is quantitatively significant: the diffusion coefficients are comparable to or exceed QLT predictions at energies where the gyroradius--thickness resonance condition is well satisfied.

Our model is necessarily simplified and several important effects have been neglected. First, we do not include realistic waiting-time distributions between current sheets. The observed solar wind exhibits clustered distributions of discontinuities @borovskyContributionStrongDiscontinuities2010, and heavy-tailed waiting-time distributions could give rise to anomalous (non-Fickian) transport regimes @giacaloneAnomalousCosmicRays2022@effenbergerOpenIssuesNongaussian2025, where the mean-square displacement grows as $t^alpha$ with $alpha eq.not 1$. Our use of a fixed inter-sheet spacing $d$ precludes this possibility and may underestimate the variability of transport in the real solar wind.

Second, we neglect the systematic evolution of the heliospheric magnetic field with radial distance and the resulting changes in current-sheet configurations experienced by propagating particles. As particles travel from the Sun to 1 AU, they encounter current sheets whose statistical properties (thickness, shear angle, field strength) evolve with heliocentric distance. A self-consistent treatment would couple the transport model to a radial profile of current sheet statistics.

Third, the heliosphere is inherently three-dimensional @effenbergerDiffusionApproximationTelegraph2014, and actual current sheets exhibit complex three-dimensional structures---including reconnection sites, flux ropes, and plasmoid chains @pezziCurrentSheetsPlasmoids2021 --- that are not captured in our one-dimensional force-free model. These structures may introduce additional scattering channels and modify the perpendicular transport efficiency.

Despite these simplifications, our results provide some of the first quantitative evidence that current sheets play a substantial role in modulating energetic-particle transport. Because current sheets constitute a major component of solar-wind turbulence @malandrakiCurrentSheetsMagnetic2019 and contribute significantly to magnetic field fluctuations @borovskyContributionStrongDiscontinuities2010, accounting for their effects is essential for developing accurate transport models @whitmanReviewSolarEnergetic2023. The strong energy dependence of the perpendicular-to-parallel diffusion ratio, in particular, may have important implications for interpreting multi-spacecraft SEP observations and for forecasting radiation environments in the inner heliosphere. Future work should aim to incorporate more realistic descriptions of current-sheet geometries, dynamics, and spatial distributions to better quantify and constrain their influence on particle propagation.